Iraqi Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi is considering sending army units to sites in the Kurdistan Region of Iraq that the government says are being used to smuggle oil, Iraqi sources familiar with the matter told MBN. According to political sources close to al-Zaidi, "the prime minister sought in recent days to move army units toward&hellip;
Al-Zaidi sent Popular Mobilization Forces Commission chief Faleh al-Fayyad to the region carrying a message urging local authorities to take control of the smuggling issue and cooperate with the federal government, the sources said. It was not immediately clear what response, if any, Kurdish authorities had given.
The move is part of Baghdad’s efforts, announced on Tuesday, to transfer control of the Kurdish region’s oil exports, border crossings and oil fields to federal authorities.

Shafaq News- Basra Hundreds of unemployed graduates and Education Ministry contract workers shut down the provincial government headquarters in Basra, Iraq's southern oil hub, on Monday, in two sep
More than 17,000 graduates who have waited years for government employment staged their eighteenth protest, demanding positions under recently allocated education, electricity and water contracts, as well as other public-sector jobs in the province, protest representative Nawaf al-Tamimi told Shafaq News. &ldquo;Only about 400 graduates were appointed; some posts went to candidates chosen through favoritism, including people with no qualifications beyond basic literacy.&rdquo;
At the second protest, hundreds of education contractors demanded salaries that have gone unpaid for more than five months. "Our repeated demands have produced no solutions," their representative, Ali al-Mohammadawi, told Shafaq News.

